ALREADY-MITIGATED LENS (applied, and it is what caps severity): no test covers this -- `grep -rn "proacl|has_function_privilege" tests/ scripts/` hits only compare-database-posture.mjs:175. The mitigation is inside the functions themselves: create_organization_with_owner (20260829460000:76), accept_organization_invitation (20260829380000:194), accept_sharing_grant_invitation (20260903100000:58), record_shared_data_consumption (20260829360000:511, :744) and record_declaration_filed (20260903110000:88) each open with `if auth.uid() is null then raise`; list_org_members raises "Not a member of this organization"; sharing_counterparty_org_names filters through app.user_org_ids(), empty for anon. An anon PostgREST JWT carries no `sub`, so all seven fail closed. That matches the finder's own "NONE reachable today".

SEVERITY: the finding is real and is exactly the failure class under hunt (true of a database whose migrations post-date the last seed, false of a freshly built one), but it is a defence-in-depth + documentation + verification-coverage defect with no reachable exploit, by the finder's own admission and my own reading of all seven bodies. HIGH implies exploitable security impact that does not exist; MEDIUM. Two caveats worth carrying forward rather than suppressing: (1) the structural half -- the seed's default ACL anon-grants EXECUTE on the NEXT SECURITY DEFINER RPC any migration adds, before anyone reviews it -- is the part that can become HIGH without anyone editing seed.sql; (2) on the hosted platform the equivalent exposure may pre-exist independently of the seed: on the local instance `pg_default_acl` already shows `supabase_admin | public | f | {postgres=X,anon=X,authenticated=X,service_role=X}` from the platform bootstrap, though for `postgres` (the role migrations run as) it is only `{postgres=X/postgres}`. I did not query production to settle which applies there, since it does not change whether the seed/doc/comparator defect exists.

